.TH DARPA-SPILL-CLIENT 1 "September 24, 2026" "darpa_spillserver 1.3.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
darpa-spill-client \- query and administer a DARPA Spill Information Server over HTTP
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B darpa-spill-client
.RI [ GLOBAL-OPTIONS ]
.I COMMAND
.RI [ COMMAND-OPTIONS ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B darpa-spill-client
drives a running
.BR darpa-spill-server (1)
through its HTTP API. Where
.BR darpa-spill-query (1)
reads an archive file directly, this program needs only network access to a
server, so it works from any host and never touches the database.
.PP
Every request goes through the Python client library,
.BR darpa_spillserver.client ,
which uses only the Python standard library for HTTP.
.PP
.BR darpa-spill-client-cpp (1)
implements the same commands, options and output rules on top of the C++
library
.BR darpa_spill_client (3).
For the same invocation both write byte-identical standard output; the test
suite checks this. The contract is written down in
.I docs/CLIENT.md
in the source distribution.
.SS Quoting hex signals
Signals are written with a leading dollar sign,
.BR $74 ", " $8F .
A shell expands an unquoted
.B $74
to nothing, so quote it:
.BR "\-\-signal '$74'" .
Registry names such as
.B numi
work unquoted.
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
Global options come before the command.
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-config " " \fIFILE\fR
YAML client configuration file (see
.BR CONFIGURATION ).
.TP
.BI \-\-env\-file " FILE"
\&.env file to read.
.TP
.BR \-u ", " \-\-url " " \fIURL\fR
Server base URL,
.B http://
or
.BR https:// .
Default \fBhttp://localhost:8080\fR.
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-timeout " " \fISECONDS\fR
Per-request timeout. Default \fB30.0\fR.
.TP
.BI \-\-admin\-token " TOKEN"
Admin token, sent in the \fBX\-Admin\-Token\fR header. Needed only by
.B admin\-check
and the
.B source
commands. Prefer \fB\-\-admin\-token\-file\fR, since a command line is visible
to other users.
.TP
.BI \-\-admin\-token\-file " FILE"
File whose first line is the admin token.
.TP
.BI \-\-ca\-file " FILE"
PEM bundle to verify an https server against.
.TP
.BR \-k ", " \-\-insecure
Accept any TLS certificate.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-format " " \fIFORMAT\fR
One of \fBtable\fR (default), \fBjson\fR, \fBcsv\fR. See
.BR OUTPUT .
.TP
.BI \-\-tz " ZONE"
Zone for time inputs that carry none, sent as \fBtz=\fR on
.BR convert ", " events " and " export .
.TP
.B \-\-print\-config
Print the merged client configuration as YAML and exit. The token itself is
shown as \fB'***'\fR.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print usage and exit. Each command also accepts \fB\-h\fR.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B health
Liveness check,
.IR "GET /api/health" .
.TP
.B status
Configuration, archive extent and ingest health,
.IR "GET /api/status" .
.TP
.B sources
The TDUs the server records from,
.IR "GET /api/sources" .
.TP
.B signals
Every accelerator signal the server decodes,
.IR "GET /api/signals" .
.TP
.B types
The decoded spill types,
.IR "GET /api/types" .
.TP
.BI convert " TIME"
Render one instant as NOvA ticks, UNIX, UTC and GPS,
.IR "GET /api/time/convert" .
.TP
.B time\-help
The accepted time expressions,
.IR "GET /api/time/help" .
.TP
.B latest \fR[\fB\-\-signal\fR \fISIG\fR] [\fB\-\-source\fR \fINAME\fR]...
The newest stored event,
.IR "GET /api/latest" .
.TP
.B events \fR[\fISELECTION\fR] [\fB\-\-limit\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-offset\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-desc\fR] [\fB\-\-columns\fR \fILIST\fR]
One page of matching events,
.IR "GET /api/events" .
.B \-\-desc
sends \fBorder=desc\fR (newest first).
.B \-\-columns
is a comma-separated subset of the output columns listed in
.BR darpa-spill-query (1).
.TP
.B export \fR[\fISELECTION\fR] [\fB\-\-columns\fR \fILIST\fR] [\fB\-o\fR \fIFILE\fR]
Every matching event, streamed without paging,
.IR "GET /api/export" .
Memory use does not grow with the size of the export. Written to
.I FILE
with \fB\-o\fR/\fB\-\-output\fR, else to standard output.
.TP
.B admin\-check
Succeeds only with valid admin credentials,
.IR "GET /api/admin/check" .
.TP
.BI "source enable " NAME
.TQ
.BI "source disable " NAME
Start or stop recording from a source,
.IR "PATCH /api/sources/NAME" .
.TP
.BI "source set\-url " "NAME URL"
Point a source at a different TDU.
.TP
.BI "source reset " NAME
Discard runtime changes and return the source to the server's configuration
file,
.IR "POST /api/sources/NAME/reset" .
.PP
.I SELECTION
is any of
.BI \-\-start " TIME" ,
.BI \-\-end " TIME" ,
.BI \-\-signal " SIG" ,
.BI \-\-type " TYPE"
and
.BI \-\-source " NAME" ;
the last three may be repeated and repeats form a union. The range is
half-open,
.IR "start <= t < end" .
Times take every form listed by
.BR time\-help ,
such as
.BR now ", " today ", " \-2h ", " 09:15 ", " 2026\-07\-01T09:15:00Z ", " nova: \fITICKS\fR.
.SH OUTPUT
.TP
.B json
The response body exactly as the server sent it, followed by a newline if it
does not already end in one.
.TP
.B csv
For
.B events
and
.BR export ,
the server's CSV verbatim, including its
.B #
comment header recording the query and any caveat.
For
.BR sources ", " signals " and " types ,
client-rendered CSV of the table columns below. Other commands print as
.BR table .
.TP
.B table
.BR sources ,
.B signals
and
.B types
print as aligned tables with columns
.RB ( name ", " base_url ", " enabled ", " overridden ", " events ),
.RB ( hex ", " name ", " spill_type_name ", " description )
and
.RB ( value ", " name ", " ambiguous ", " signals ).
.B events
asks the server for CSV and lays it out as a table, by default with columns
.BR utc_string ", " gps_week ", " gps_tow_exact ", " signal ", "
.BR spill_type_name ", " event_number ", " source ;
each warning in the CSV header is written to standard error as
.BR "warning: ..." .
Every other command prints one
.B "key: value"
line per leaf of the JSON response, nested keys joined with
.B .
and list elements by index
.RB ( sources.0.name ).
.PP
In tables and key/value lines, \fBtrue\fR/\fBfalse\fR print as
\fByes\fR/\fBno\fR, \fBnull\fR as \fB\-\fR, a non-integer number with up to
six decimals (trailing zeros removed), and a list of values joined with
commas.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Settings are resolved in this order, highest first:
command line > environment > .env > YAML file > defaults.
.PP
The YAML file holds a single
.B client:
section; unknown keys are an error:
.PP
.RS
.EX
client:
  url: http://localhost:8080
  timeout: 30.0
  admin_token: ""
  admin_token_file: ""
  ca_file: ""
  verify_tls: true
  format: table      # table | json | csv
  timezone: ""
.EE
.RE
.PP
It is \fB\-\-config\fR, else \fB$DARPA_SPILL_CLIENT_CONFIG\fR, else the first
of the files under
.B FILES
that exists.
.PP
The \fB.env\fR file is \fB\-\-env\-file\fR, else \fB$DARPA_SPILL_ENV_FILE\fR,
else
.I ./.env
if it exists. It holds
.IB KEY = VALUE
lines; blank lines and
.B #
comments are skipped, a leading
.B export
is ignored, and one layer of matching quotes is removed. Only
.B DARPA_SPILL_
variables are used, and one also set in the real environment keeps the
environment's value.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.BI DARPA_SPILL_CLIENT_ KEY
Sets the client key
.I KEY
in upper case:
.BR DARPA_SPILL_CLIENT_URL ", " DARPA_SPILL_CLIENT_TIMEOUT ", "
.BR DARPA_SPILL_CLIENT_ADMIN_TOKEN ", " DARPA_SPILL_CLIENT_ADMIN_TOKEN_FILE ", "
.BR DARPA_SPILL_CLIENT_CA_FILE ", " DARPA_SPILL_CLIENT_VERIFY_TLS ", "
.BR DARPA_SPILL_CLIENT_FORMAT ", " DARPA_SPILL_CLIENT_TIMEZONE .
Booleans accept
.BR 1/0 ", " true/false ", " yes/no " and " on/off .
.TP
.B DARPA_SPILL_CLIENT_CONFIG
The YAML file, as \fB\-\-config\fR.
.TP
.B DARPA_SPILL_ENV_FILE
The \fB.env\fR file, as \fB\-\-env\-file\fR.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ./config/spillclient.yaml
.TQ
.I ~/.config/darpa\-spillserver/spillclient.yaml
.TQ
.I /etc/darpa\-spillserver/spillclient.yaml
Configuration files, searched in this order. On Windows the last is
.IR %PROGRAMDATA%\e darpa\-spillserver\e spillclient.yaml .
.TP
.I ./.env
Optional \fB.env\fR file.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
The server answered with an HTTP error. Standard error gets
.B "error: HTTP" \fISTATUS\fB:\fR \fIMESSAGE\fR
and, when the server gave one,
.BR "hint: " \fIHINT\fR.
.TP
.B 2
A usage or configuration error.
.TP
.B 3
The server could not be reached: connection refused, DNS, TLS or timeout.
.SH EXAMPLES
Today's NuMI spills from the Near Detector gateway:
.PP
.RS
.EX
darpa\-spill\-client \-u http://novadaq\-near\-gateway\-01.fnal.gov:8080 \e
    events \-\-start today \-\-signal '$74'
.EE
.RE
.PP
The same as CSV, saved to a file:
.PP
.RS
.EX
darpa\-spill\-client \-f csv events \-\-start today \-\-signal '$74' > numi.csv
.EE
.RE
.PP
A whole day of 1 Hz events, streamed:
.PP
.RS
.EX
darpa\-spill\-client export \-\-start 2026\-07\-01 \-\-end 2026\-07\-01 \e
    \-\-signal '$8f' \-o one\-hertz.csv
.EE
.RE
.PP
Check that the server is up, for a monitoring script:
.PP
.RS
.EX
darpa\-spill\-client \-u https://gateway:8443 \-\-ca\-file site.pem health
.EE
.RE
.PP
Stop recording from a TDU, then restore it:
.PP
.RS
.EX
darpa\-spill\-client \-\-admin\-token\-file ~/.spill\-admin \e
    source disable tdu\-near\-master\-ppc\-02
darpa\-spill\-client \-\-admin\-token\-file ~/.spill\-admin \e
    source reset tdu\-near\-master\-ppc\-02
.EE
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR darpa-spill-client-cpp (1),
.BR darpa-spill-server (1),
.BR darpa-spill-query (1),
.BR darpa_spill_client (3),
.BR darpa_spillserver (3)
